<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    *** BUNDLED TRUST ANCHORS — the fix for what sealed machine 8626020618.

    On 2026-07-29 the Railway certificate renewed onto Let's Encrypt's Generation Y
    hierarchy. The tablet's trust store had no Root YE, the chain it was served
    stopped there, and every HTTPS call failed at once: config poll, command poll
    and the OTA path that would have delivered the fix. With Lock Task active there
    was no route in at all and it cost a factory reset.

    The lesson is not "old device". Root YE only entered trust stores in 2026, so
    brand-new hardware would have been hit too. The real problem is that a kiosk
    with a FROZEN TRUST STORE cannot be allowed to depend on that store for the one
    path that ships its own fixes.

    So we ship the roots ourselves. Note src="system" is listed FIRST and stays:
    these are ADDITIONS, not a replacement. Everything that validates today keeps
    validating (the Squarespace CDN images on the info sheet, for instance, which we
    deliberately did not bundle a root for — their failure mode is a cosmetic
    fallback to the typographic panel, not a stopped machine).

    WHAT'S HERE AND WHY:

      GTS Root R1-R4     api.agvending.is is Google Trust Services (WE1 -> GTS Root
                         R4). All four are bundled so a rotation between GTS roots
                         can't strand us. Anchoring on R4 directly also makes us
                         independent of the GlobalSign cross-sign the server
                         currently sends — which matters, because GlobalSign Root CA
                         expires January 2028 and has already been dropped from
                         current trust bundles.

      ISRG Root X1/X2    Let's Encrypt, older generations. Still in the path for
                         *.r2.dev product images and the Railway host.

      Root YE            THE ONE WHOSE ABSENCE CAUSED THE OUTAGE. Bundled in its
                         X2-cross-signed form (CA:TRUE, valid to Sept 2032), which
                         serves as an anchor perfectly well. Verified before
                         shipping: the exact failing chain — leaf -> YE1 -> Root YE
                         with no cross-signs — validates against this anchor alone.

    Cleartext stays forbidden. It was never permitted, and an http:// apkUrl must
    not become an accidental way around any of this.

    WHEN THESE EXPIRE (2032 for Root YE, 2035-2036 for the rest) a build must ship
    replacements. That is a long fuse, not an infinite one.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="@raw/gts_root_r1" />
            <certificates src="@raw/gts_root_r2" />
            <certificates src="@raw/gts_root_r3" />
            <certificates src="@raw/gts_root_r4" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_x1" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_x2" />
            <certificates src="@raw/isrg_root_ye_cross" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
